{"step_index":66,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-11-17T09:10:50Z","content":"Created At: 2026-11-17T14:40:50+05:30\nCompleted At: 2026-11-17T14:40:50+05:30\nCreated At: 2026-11-17T14:40:50+05:30\nCompleted At: 2026-11-17T14:40:50+05:30\nShowing lines 1-38 of 38\n1: # Kezza Agent platform: repository audit\n2: \n3: ## What was inspected\n4: \n5: - `Kezza Agent App` on Desktop: a standalone Expo Router client on Expo SDK 57 / React Native 0.86.3. It currently has OTP login, agent registration, a referral summary/list, lead creation, and an agent commission screen.\n6: - The client has no backend, migration directory, admin website, or database schema of its own. Its `.env` file is present locally and was not read or copied.\n7: - `Kezza_mobile-main` is a separate Expo + FastAPI project. Its backend has existing user/referral/payment/patient-points/commission models, four Alembic revisions, and a small admin API/Expo screen. That lineage belongs to the existing application and must not be replayed into the new database without review.\n8: - The reference architecture image is historical and points to `kezza_db`; it is not the target database for this platform.\n9: - PostgreSQL is installed in the environment, but `pg_isready -h 127.0.0.1 -p 5432` returned “no response”. A read-only `psql` attempt was blocked by the environment. The existence, owner, and migration state of `kezza_rewards_db` therefore remain unverified; no database was created or modified.\n10: - The two standalone Markdown prompt files are identical. The ZIP package includes the same working specification, the 24-page product/engineering guide, database setup notes, and technical screenshots. The photo index explicitly says those screenshots are not approved app UI designs.\n11: \n12: ## Target architecture\n13: \n14: Keep the existing Desktop apps untouched. Extend the standalone Kezza Agent client as a small monorepo:\n15: \n16: ```text\n17: kezza-agent-app/\n18:   src/                 Expo agent mobile client\n19:   backend/             FastAPI + SQLAlchemy + Alembic for kezza_rewards_db\n20:   admin/               responsive TypeScript admin portal\n21:   docs/                audit, policy decisions, setup and handover\n22: ```\n23: \n24: The mobile and admin clients use the new FastAPI API. The API owns the database, OTP/session security, roles, referral attribution, payment review, point rules, ledger, cycle close, audit history, and in-app notifications. No client may set a balance or approve its own claim.\n25: \n26: ## Build order\n27: \n28: 1. Establish the clean `kezza_rewards_db` schema and safe `.env.example`; keep all existing DB URLs out of the new project.\n29: 2. Implement identity/invitation, branches/service catalog, referrals and consent capture.\n30: 3. Implement payment claims, reviewer transitions, partial approvals and transactional reward ledger posting.\n31: 4. Implement wallet/cycle preview and guarded cycle close/expiry operations.\n32: 5. Connect the mobile app to real APIs and add the navigation/screens in the specification.\n33: 6. Build the admin portal for agent/branch/catalog/claim/reward/cycle/feedback/audit operations.\n34: 7. Add authorization and business-rule coverage, type/lint/build checks, and safe runbooks.\n35: \n36: ## Environment constraints\n37: \n38: This Codex workspace can write under its project directory, not under `/Users/ravi/Desktop`. Work in this folder and provide a clean source ZIP for the Desktop app. The actual Desktop `.env`, both Desktop project folders, and the existing `kezza_db` remain untouched. Applying migrations will require PostgreSQL to be running and a later read-only preflight confirming `kezza_rewards_db` does not already contain data."}
